1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,605
Welcome to Module 6.

2
00:00:02,955 --> 00:00:06,735
Your agents can now think
in a loop and use tools safely.

3
00:00:07,085 --> 00:00:10,046
But close the program,
and they forget everything.

4
00:00:10,396 --> 00:00:14,410
Even inside one chat, they
only remember what you send them.

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:20,741
By the end of this module, you'll build
a persistent study companion: it remembers

6
00:00:20,741 --> 00:00:26,795
your name, your goals and what you find
hard, across days, in a real database, with

7
00:00:26,795 --> 00:00:28,034
privacy controls.

8
00:00:29,834 --> 00:00:34,210
In this lesson, you'll learn
what stateless means for a model API.

9
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:39,008
You'll prove it, by asking the
same question with and without history.

10
00:00:39,608 --> 00:00:42,380
You'll build a chat loop
that keeps the conversation.

11
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:46,461
And you'll meet the four
kinds of memory an agent can have.

12
00:00:48,261 --> 00:00:50,446
Let's start with an everyday picture.

13
00:00:50,796 --> 00:00:54,354
Imagine a brilliant
tutor, with one strange problem.

14
00:00:54,704 --> 00:00:58,174
The moment you leave the
room, they forget you completely.

15
00:00:58,524 --> 00:01:00,979
Your name, your last lesson, everything.

16
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:03,052
You can still get great help.

17
00:01:03,402 --> 00:01:08,161
You just have to bring your notebook
every time, and show them what you covered

18
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:08,583
before.

19
00:01:09,183 --> 00:01:10,790
The tutor is the model.

20
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,562
Very smart, but it
remembers nothing between visits.

21
00:01:15,162 --> 00:01:19,585
The notebook is the list of
messages you send with every request.

22
00:01:20,185 --> 00:01:24,670
And carrying the notebook is
your code's job, on every single call.

23
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:26,918
That's what stateless means.

24
00:01:27,268 --> 00:01:30,073
Each request to the API stands alone.

25
00:01:30,423 --> 00:01:33,830
The server doesn't keep
your conversation between calls.

26
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:39,075
If the model seems to remember, it's
because your code sent the history again.

27
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:42,351
Let's prove it with code.

28
00:01:42,701 --> 00:01:46,430
The code is on the left, and
what it printed is on the right.

29
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:50,368
As in every module, no API key is needed.

30
00:01:50,718 --> 00:01:55,195
A scripted fake plays the model,
and the first output line says so.

31
00:01:55,545 --> 00:02:01,110
The important thing: like a real
model, the fake only knows what is inside the

32
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:01,680
request.

33
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:07,811
This small function sends a list of
messages, and returns the text of the reply.

34
00:02:08,411 --> 00:02:09,206
Call one.

35
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:11,752
We say: hi, my name is Anuj.

36
00:02:12,352 --> 00:02:17,392
Call two is a brand new request,
with only the question: what's my name?

37
00:02:17,742 --> 00:02:19,798
And the answer is: I don't know.

38
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:21,927
Nothing in this request says it.

39
00:02:22,277 --> 00:02:23,497
Call one is gone.

40
00:02:24,097 --> 00:02:29,681
Call three sends the earlier messages
again: our introduction, the reply, and then

41
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:30,566
the question.

42
00:02:30,916 --> 00:02:33,843
Now the answer is: your name is Anuj.

43
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:35,943
Same model, same question.

44
00:02:36,293 --> 00:02:38,376
The only difference is what we sent.

45
00:02:40,176 --> 00:02:42,600
Now, how does a normal chat work, then?

46
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,429
The first request has
one message: the user's.

47
00:02:46,779 --> 00:02:48,947
The API answers and forgets.

48
00:02:49,547 --> 00:02:55,249
For the second request, your code sends
three messages: the first user message, the

49
00:02:55,249 --> 00:02:58,271
assistant's reply,
and the new user message.

50
00:02:58,871 --> 00:03:00,850
The third request sends five.

51
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:06,852
The conversation grows by two messages
every turn, and the whole thing travels with

52
00:03:06,852 --> 00:03:07,805
every request.

53
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:13,345
Keep this picture in mind: it's the
reason for everything in the next two lessons.

54
00:03:15,145 --> 00:03:16,732
Here's a real chat loop.

55
00:03:17,082 --> 00:03:20,464
One list, history,
holds the whole conversation.

56
00:03:21,064 --> 00:03:25,389
Each line of input becomes a
user message, appended to the list.

57
00:03:25,739 --> 00:03:30,089
For a repeatable demo, the three
lines come from a small text file.

58
00:03:30,689 --> 00:03:33,025
Then we send the whole list, every time.

59
00:03:33,625 --> 00:03:36,596
And we append the reply
as an assistant message.

60
00:03:36,946 --> 00:03:41,580
Forget this line, and the model
never sees its own earlier answers.

61
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:46,425
Turn one: two messages stored,
and thirteen input tokens sent.

62
00:03:47,025 --> 00:03:48,632
Turn two: what's my name?

63
00:03:48,982 --> 00:03:51,765
Anuj, because the
history went with the request.

64
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:56,453
And look at the input
tokens: thirteen, sixty-one, ninety.

65
00:03:56,803 --> 00:03:58,290
Every turn sends more.

66
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:04,108
You pay for input tokens on every call,
and the model has a maximum it can read.

67
00:04:04,458 --> 00:04:07,524
That's the problem we
solve in lessons two and three.

68
00:04:09,324 --> 00:04:12,403
Let's name the kinds of
memory an agent can have.

69
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:16,130
First, conversation
history: the messages list.

70
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,586
Exact, but it grows every turn.

71
00:04:19,186 --> 00:04:23,312
Second, a short-term window:
send only the last few turns.

72
00:04:23,662 --> 00:04:25,826
Cheap, but old details fall out.

73
00:04:26,426 --> 00:04:32,041
Third, summary memory: old turns
compressed into a short summary that keeps the

74
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:32,396
gist.

75
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:38,885
And fourth, long-term memory: facts
saved in a database, which survive a restart,

76
00:04:38,885 --> 00:04:41,357
and a brand new chat the next day.

77
00:04:43,157 --> 00:04:44,624
Where does each one live?

78
00:04:44,974 --> 00:04:49,705
History lives in a Python list,
and it's lost when the program exits.

79
00:04:50,305 --> 00:04:53,085
The window is just the
part of the list you send.

80
00:04:53,435 --> 00:04:56,383
A turn is lost from the
request when it slides out.

81
00:04:56,983 --> 00:05:00,376
The summary is a string,
sent with the system prompt.

82
00:05:00,726 --> 00:05:04,121
Details are lost if they
don't make it into the summary.

83
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:08,242
And long-term memory
lives in a database file.

84
00:05:08,592 --> 00:05:11,460
It's only lost when
you delete it, on purpose.

85
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:15,099
Pause the video and try this.

86
00:05:15,449 --> 00:05:19,072
The history so far is:
user says, I love chess.

87
00:05:19,422 --> 00:05:20,960
Assistant says, nice.

88
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:25,450
Then your code sends a new
request with only: what do I love?

89
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,127
What can the model answer, and why?

90
00:05:28,477 --> 00:05:30,322
And what would you change in the code?

91
00:05:36,922 --> 00:05:37,955
It can't know.

92
00:05:38,305 --> 00:05:43,476
The request doesn't mention chess,
and the API keeps nothing between calls.

93
00:05:43,826 --> 00:05:49,131
The fix: append every message to the
history list, and send the whole list each

94
00:05:49,131 --> 00:05:49,467
time.

95
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:52,408
Interview corner.

96
00:05:52,758 --> 00:05:56,297
Does the Claude API
remember earlier messages?

97
00:05:58,897 --> 00:06:00,327
A good answer: no.

98
00:06:00,677 --> 00:06:02,846
The Messages API is stateless.

99
00:06:03,196 --> 00:06:04,948
Each request stands alone.

100
00:06:05,298 --> 00:06:11,027
A conversation works because your code
resends the history, the user and assistant

101
00:06:11,027 --> 00:06:13,682
messages in order, with every request.

102
00:06:14,032 --> 00:06:16,890
The trap is saying the
model remembers your chat.

103
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:20,420
Any memory is data
your code stores, and sends.

104
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:24,702
A follow-up: why not just
keep the whole history forever?

105
00:06:27,302 --> 00:06:28,992
Because of cost and limits.

106
00:06:29,342 --> 00:06:35,101
Every turn resends everything, so input
tokens, and the time to answer, grow with

107
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:36,310
the conversation.

108
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:39,920
And eventually, it
won't fit the context window.

109
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:44,644
So you need a strategy:
windows, summaries, and long-term facts.

110
00:06:44,994 --> 00:06:47,494
The trap is thinking
it's only about money.

111
00:06:47,844 --> 00:06:50,873
Long contexts also
make every reply slower.

112
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:53,532
Quick check.

113
00:06:53,882 --> 00:06:57,752
Your chatbot forgets what
the user said two messages ago.

114
00:06:58,102 --> 00:06:59,752
What's the most likely bug?

115
00:07:05,952 --> 00:07:09,642
The code doesn't append every
message to the history it sends.

116
00:07:09,992 --> 00:07:12,556
The model only sees what's in the request.

117
00:07:14,356 --> 00:07:15,446
Let's recap.

118
00:07:15,796 --> 00:07:17,447
The API is stateless.

119
00:07:17,797 --> 00:07:19,548
Each request stands alone.

120
00:07:20,148 --> 00:07:24,159
A conversation is your code
resending the history, every time.

121
00:07:24,759 --> 00:07:27,411
Append the user's message, and the reply.

122
00:07:28,011 --> 00:07:33,245
And the history grows every turn,
which costs money and time, and hits limits.

123
00:07:33,845 --> 00:07:35,974
Next lesson: short-term memory.

124
00:07:36,324 --> 00:07:41,984
You'll measure how fast the
conversation grows, meet the context window, and keep

125
00:07:41,984 --> 00:07:45,897
only the turns that matter,
without breaking tool calls.

126
00:07:47,695 --> 00:07:53,296
In lesson one, you saw that a
conversation is a list you resend every time, and that

127
00:07:53,296 --> 00:07:54,430
it keeps growing.

128
00:07:54,780 --> 00:08:00,344
In this lesson, you'll measure that
growth, learn what the context window is, and

129
00:08:00,344 --> 00:08:05,771
keep the conversation small with a
sliding window, without breaking tool calls.

130
00:08:07,571 --> 00:08:10,676
You'll explain tokens,
and the context window.

131
00:08:11,276 --> 00:08:14,663
Measure how a
conversation grows, turn by turn.

132
00:08:15,263 --> 00:08:18,809
Keep only the last few
turns, with a sliding window.

133
00:08:19,409 --> 00:08:23,541
And cut only at turn
boundaries, so tool calls stay valid.

134
00:08:25,341 --> 00:08:26,481
First, a picture.

135
00:08:26,831 --> 00:08:30,117
A classroom whiteboard
holds only so much writing.

136
00:08:30,467 --> 00:08:35,196
When it's full, the teacher must
erase something before writing anything new.

137
00:08:35,546 --> 00:08:38,649
The context window is
the model's whiteboard.

138
00:08:38,999 --> 00:08:44,932
It's everything the model can read in one
request: the system prompt, the tools, the

139
00:08:44,932 --> 00:08:47,262
messages, and room for its reply.

140
00:08:47,862 --> 00:08:49,520
It's measured in tokens.

141
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:53,344
A token is a piece of
text, a few characters long.

142
00:08:53,694 --> 00:08:56,486
In English, roughly
three quarters of a word.

143
00:08:57,086 --> 00:09:01,159
The window size is the most
tokens one request can hold.

144
00:09:01,759 --> 00:09:05,692
And you pay for every input
token you send, on every call.

145
00:09:06,292 --> 00:09:12,360
Today's Claude models have very large
context windows, hundreds of thousands of

146
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:12,898
tokens.

147
00:09:13,248 --> 00:09:14,388
So why worry?

148
00:09:14,738 --> 00:09:20,426
Because long before you hit the limit,
a growing conversation costs more on every

149
00:09:20,426 --> 00:09:22,814
turn, and every reply gets slower.

150
00:09:24,614 --> 00:09:25,689
Let's measure it.

151
00:09:26,039 --> 00:09:29,570
The code is on the left,
and its output is on the right.

152
00:09:30,170 --> 00:09:32,661
We build a conversation of five turns.

153
00:09:33,011 --> 00:09:37,724
Each turn is a short question,
and a long answer, like real tutoring.

154
00:09:38,324 --> 00:09:40,472
After each turn we print the size.

155
00:09:40,822 --> 00:09:44,102
About a hundred and
fifty tokens more every turn.

156
00:09:44,452 --> 00:09:47,537
And remember, the whole
thing is sent on every call.

157
00:09:47,887 --> 00:09:49,944
That's the cost of perfect memory.

158
00:09:50,544 --> 00:09:53,495
Now the simplest fix: a sliding window.

159
00:09:53,845 --> 00:09:56,016
We keep only the last two turns.

160
00:09:56,616 --> 00:10:01,210
Four messages, about two hundred
and ninety tokens, less than half.

161
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:04,742
The oldest message kept is: explain loops.

162
00:10:05,342 --> 00:10:06,710
But here's the price.

163
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:08,703
We ask: what's my name?

164
00:10:09,053 --> 00:10:11,110
And the answer is: I don't know.

165
00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:15,514
The name was in turn one, and
turn one slid out of the window.

166
00:10:17,314 --> 00:10:19,919
There are two common
ways to size a window.

167
00:10:20,269 --> 00:10:22,851
By turns: keep the last N turns.

168
00:10:23,201 --> 00:10:25,462
It's simple, and easy to reason about.

169
00:10:25,812 --> 00:10:29,280
But one turn with a huge
answer can still be too big.

170
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:34,553
Or by tokens: keep as many
recent turns as fit a token budget.

171
00:10:34,903 --> 00:10:37,204
That keeps the size under a known limit.

172
00:10:37,554 --> 00:10:43,953
To count tokens you can estimate, about
four characters per token, or ask the API

173
00:10:43,953 --> 00:10:47,114
exactly, with its count tokens endpoint.

174
00:10:48,914 --> 00:10:50,646
Here's the project's version.

175
00:10:50,996 --> 00:10:55,393
First, a rough token estimate:
about four characters per token.

176
00:10:55,743 --> 00:10:57,858
Good enough to decide when to trim.

177
00:10:58,208 --> 00:11:02,436
For exact numbers, use the
API's count tokens endpoint.

178
00:11:03,036 --> 00:11:05,136
Now, where do developers go wrong?

179
00:11:05,486 --> 00:11:06,982
Cutting in the wrong place.

180
00:11:07,332 --> 00:11:11,339
A turn starts with a user
message whose content is plain text.

181
00:11:11,689 --> 00:11:17,031
Tool results are also sent with the user
role, but they're a list of blocks, so they

182
00:11:17,031 --> 00:11:18,239
don't start a turn.

183
00:11:20,039 --> 00:11:25,384
The conversation class holds the
messages, and a summary we'll use next lesson.

184
00:11:25,734 --> 00:11:28,031
Its size is the estimate of both.

185
00:11:28,631 --> 00:11:31,790
This method finds the
index where each turn starts.

186
00:11:32,390 --> 00:11:36,066
And the window starts at the
turn start, so many turns back.

187
00:11:36,416 --> 00:11:41,515
That guarantees a tool use block
and its tool result are never separated.

188
00:11:43,315 --> 00:11:45,970
Let's dry run it on a
chat with a tool call.

189
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:50,166
Message zero, the user's
own words: a turn starts here.

190
00:11:50,766 --> 00:11:53,820
Message one: the
assistant asks for a tool.

191
00:11:54,420 --> 00:11:56,474
Message two: the tool result.

192
00:11:56,824 --> 00:11:59,102
User role, but not a turn start.

193
00:11:59,702 --> 00:12:01,526
Message three: the reply.

194
00:12:02,126 --> 00:12:04,053
Message four starts turn two.

195
00:12:04,403 --> 00:12:07,080
With keep turns two,
the window starts here.

196
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:09,635
And everything after it is kept.

197
00:12:11,435 --> 00:12:12,599
Pause and try it.

198
00:12:12,949 --> 00:12:14,577
Here are eight messages.

199
00:12:14,927 --> 00:12:19,500
Message three is a tool use,
and message four is its tool result.

200
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:23,256
Which messages does a
window of two turns keep?

201
00:12:29,856 --> 00:12:32,677
Turns start at messages zero, two and six.

202
00:12:33,027 --> 00:12:38,801
Keeping two turns means starting at
message two, so it keeps messages two to seven,

203
00:12:38,801 --> 00:12:41,513
and the tool use stays with its result.

204
00:12:43,313 --> 00:12:44,455
Interview corner.

205
00:12:44,805 --> 00:12:49,162
What is a context window, and
how do you manage it in a long chat?

206
00:12:51,762 --> 00:12:57,858
A good answer: it's the most tokens a
model can read in one request, including the

207
00:12:57,858 --> 00:13:01,204
system prompt, tools,
messages and the reply.

208
00:13:01,554 --> 00:13:07,319
You manage it with a sliding window of
recent turns, summaries of older turns, and

209
00:13:07,319 --> 00:13:10,343
long-term facts that
you fetch when needed.

210
00:13:10,693 --> 00:13:14,692
The trap: saying a big window
means you never need to manage it.

211
00:13:15,042 --> 00:13:18,123
Cost and latency grow
long before the limit.

212
00:13:18,723 --> 00:13:19,660
Follow-up.

213
00:13:20,010 --> 00:13:22,641
Why can't you just
drop the oldest message?

214
00:13:25,241 --> 00:13:27,569
Because messages come in linked groups.

215
00:13:27,919 --> 00:13:33,692
Dropping a tool use but keeping its tool
result, or the reverse, makes the request

216
00:13:33,692 --> 00:13:34,255
invalid.

217
00:13:34,605 --> 00:13:36,461
So you cut at turn boundaries.

218
00:13:36,811 --> 00:13:42,222
The trap is forgetting that tool results
are sent with the user role, so not every

219
00:13:42,222 --> 00:13:44,004
user message starts a turn.

220
00:13:45,804 --> 00:13:46,663
Quick check.

221
00:13:47,013 --> 00:13:49,855
A sliding window
keeps the last three turns.

222
00:13:50,205 --> 00:13:51,519
What's the main risk?

223
00:13:57,719 --> 00:14:01,288
Facts from early turns,
like the user's name, are lost.

224
00:14:01,638 --> 00:14:04,090
The window keeps costs down, but forgets.

225
00:14:05,890 --> 00:14:06,980
Let's recap.

226
00:14:07,330 --> 00:14:12,703
The context window is everything one
request can hold, measured in tokens.

227
00:14:13,303 --> 00:14:17,394
A conversation grows every
turn, which costs money and time.

228
00:14:17,994 --> 00:14:23,364
A sliding window keeps the last few
turns, or as many as fit a token budget.

229
00:14:23,964 --> 00:14:28,022
And always cut where a turn
starts, so tool pairs stay together.

230
00:14:28,622 --> 00:14:30,635
Next lesson: summary memory.

231
00:14:30,985 --> 00:14:36,419
Instead of throwing old turns away,
we'll fold them into a short summary, and get

232
00:14:36,419 --> 00:14:37,358
the name back.

233
00:14:39,172 --> 00:14:44,837
In lesson two, a sliding window kept
the conversation small, but it forgot the

234
00:14:44,837 --> 00:14:45,709
user's name.

235
00:14:46,059 --> 00:14:51,306
In this lesson, instead of throwing
old turns away, you'll compress them into a

236
00:14:51,306 --> 00:14:52,767
short running summary.

237
00:14:53,117 --> 00:14:55,619
Small like a window,
but it keeps the gist.

238
00:14:57,419 --> 00:15:01,338
You'll explain summary memory,
and when it beats a plain window.

239
00:15:01,938 --> 00:15:05,655
Summarize old turns,
with one extra model call.

240
00:15:06,255 --> 00:15:09,160
Send the summary along
with the system prompt.

241
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:13,448
And know the costs: lost
details, and an extra call.

242
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:16,893
Think about a long meeting.

243
00:15:17,243 --> 00:15:20,409
Afterwards, nobody
re-reads the full transcript.

244
00:15:20,759 --> 00:15:25,395
People read the minutes: the decisions,
who agreed to what, and what's still open.

245
00:15:25,745 --> 00:15:29,102
Summary memory does the
same for a conversation.

246
00:15:29,452 --> 00:15:35,078
The old part becomes minutes, and only
the last few turns are kept word for word.

247
00:15:35,678 --> 00:15:38,419
Old turns become a short, running summary.

248
00:15:39,019 --> 00:15:43,436
Recent turns are kept exactly,
because details matter most there.

249
00:15:44,036 --> 00:15:48,800
And every request sends the summary,
the recent turns, and the new message.

250
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:53,660
Running means the summary is
updated, not rewritten from scratch.

251
00:15:54,010 --> 00:15:59,016
Each time, the model merges the old
summary with the turns that are leaving.

252
00:15:59,366 --> 00:16:02,011
So it stays short
however long the chat gets.

253
00:16:03,811 --> 00:16:05,167
Here's how it works.

254
00:16:05,517 --> 00:16:07,609
After each turn, check the size.

255
00:16:07,959 --> 00:16:10,231
Is the conversation above our limit?

256
00:16:10,831 --> 00:16:16,783
If so, split it into old turns and the
last few turns, cutting where a turn starts,

257
00:16:16,783 --> 00:16:18,002
as in lesson two.

258
00:16:18,602 --> 00:16:20,381
Then make one extra call.

259
00:16:20,731 --> 00:16:25,766
Give the model the old summary and
the old turns, and ask for a new summary.

260
00:16:26,366 --> 00:16:29,986
Finally, keep the new
summary, and drop the old turns.

261
00:16:30,336 --> 00:16:32,928
But only after the summary has arrived.

262
00:16:33,278 --> 00:16:35,531
If the call fails, nothing is lost.

263
00:16:37,331 --> 00:16:38,524
Let's see it run.

264
00:16:38,874 --> 00:16:41,033
Same five turns as lesson two.

265
00:16:41,383 --> 00:16:44,365
The code is on the
left, the output on the right.

266
00:16:44,965 --> 00:16:51,011
First, the same conversation as last
lesson: five turns, about seven hundred and

267
00:16:51,011 --> 00:16:52,069
thirty tokens.

268
00:16:52,669 --> 00:16:58,559
The summarize function builds a prompt
with the summary so far, or none, and the old

269
00:16:58,559 --> 00:17:01,433
messages written out as plain text lines.

270
00:17:02,033 --> 00:17:07,322
Then one extra model call, with a
system prompt written just for summarizing.

271
00:17:07,922 --> 00:17:13,178
Compact folds the old turns in when
we're over the limit, keeping the last two

272
00:17:13,178 --> 00:17:13,582
turns.

273
00:17:13,932 --> 00:17:19,836
Six messages folded, and the size drops
from about seven hundred and thirty tokens

274
00:17:19,836 --> 00:17:21,492
to about three hundred.

275
00:17:22,092 --> 00:17:23,225
Here's the summary.

276
00:17:23,575 --> 00:17:28,793
In offline mode the fake writes a
simple extract of what the learner said, and

277
00:17:28,793 --> 00:17:31,736
labels it, so we
never pretend it's a model.

278
00:17:32,086 --> 00:17:35,866
A real model writes a
proper summary in its own words.

279
00:17:36,466 --> 00:17:39,254
And now the question
again: what's my name?

280
00:17:39,604 --> 00:17:44,626
The summary goes into the system
prompt, and this time the answer is: Anuj.

281
00:17:44,976 --> 00:17:47,756
As small as the
window, but it kept the name.

282
00:17:49,556 --> 00:17:55,207
In the project, the summarizer's system
prompt says what to keep: facts about the

283
00:17:55,207 --> 00:17:59,323
learner, topics covered,
open questions, and promises made.

284
00:17:59,673 --> 00:18:03,347
And it sets a limit: at
most a hundred and twenty words.

285
00:18:03,697 --> 00:18:07,559
Without a limit, summaries
slowly grow back into transcripts.

286
00:18:09,359 --> 00:18:11,260
Here's compact in the project.

287
00:18:11,610 --> 00:18:15,393
Under the limit, or with too
few turns to fold, it does nothing.

288
00:18:15,993 --> 00:18:20,206
Otherwise it cuts where a turn
starts, so tool pairs stay together.

289
00:18:20,806 --> 00:18:22,084
And the order matters.

290
00:18:22,434 --> 00:18:23,664
Summarize first.

291
00:18:24,014 --> 00:18:28,762
Only when the new summary has
arrived do we drop the old messages.

292
00:18:29,112 --> 00:18:32,717
If the API call fails,
every message is still there.

293
00:18:34,517 --> 00:18:36,457
So which strategy should you use?

294
00:18:36,807 --> 00:18:41,092
Full history keeps everything
exactly, but grows every turn.

295
00:18:41,692 --> 00:18:46,025
A window keeps recent turns
exactly, but forgets older ones.

296
00:18:46,625 --> 00:18:52,878
A summary plus a window keeps the gist
of old turns and the recent turns exactly.

297
00:18:53,228 --> 00:18:57,947
It costs an extra call now and
then, and fine details can be lost.

298
00:18:58,547 --> 00:19:03,941
And for the facts that must never be
lost, like a name, we add long-term memory,

299
00:19:03,941 --> 00:19:04,750
next lesson.

300
00:19:06,550 --> 00:19:07,707
Pause and try.

301
00:19:08,057 --> 00:19:13,505
Here are some old turns from Riya: I'm
preparing for a Python interview on Friday.

302
00:19:13,855 --> 00:19:16,815
Lists are fine, decorators confuse me.

303
00:19:17,165 --> 00:19:18,791
Can you quiz me tomorrow?

304
00:19:19,391 --> 00:19:22,106
Write a summary in at
most twenty five words.

305
00:19:22,456 --> 00:19:23,714
What must it keep?

306
00:19:30,314 --> 00:19:33,997
One good summary: Riya,
Python interview on Friday.

307
00:19:34,347 --> 00:19:37,547
Comfortable with lists,
struggles with decorators.

308
00:19:37,897 --> 00:19:39,676
Promised: a quiz tomorrow.

309
00:19:40,026 --> 00:19:43,378
It keeps the fact, the goal,
the struggle, and the promise.

310
00:19:45,178 --> 00:19:46,320
Interview corner.

311
00:19:46,670 --> 00:19:49,668
How would you keep a long
running chat within budget?

312
00:19:52,268 --> 00:19:58,061
A good answer: keep the last few
turns word for word, fold older turns into a

313
00:19:58,061 --> 00:20:03,929
running summary with a dedicated
prompt, and move lasting facts into long-term

314
00:20:03,929 --> 00:20:04,455
memory.

315
00:20:04,805 --> 00:20:10,832
Summarize only when you pass a token
threshold, and drop old turns only after the

316
00:20:10,832 --> 00:20:12,097
summary succeeds.

317
00:20:12,447 --> 00:20:14,984
The trap: summarizing on every turn.

318
00:20:15,334 --> 00:20:19,175
That adds a call each time,
and the summary slowly drifts.

319
00:20:19,775 --> 00:20:21,768
And: what can go wrong with summaries?

320
00:20:24,368 --> 00:20:27,826
Details get lost,
like exact numbers or code.

321
00:20:28,176 --> 00:20:30,186
Summaries of summaries can drift.

322
00:20:30,536 --> 00:20:32,944
And the summary call costs money and time.

323
00:20:33,294 --> 00:20:38,498
You reduce that with a clear list of
what to keep, a length limit, and long-term

324
00:20:38,498 --> 00:20:41,101
facts for anything that must stay exact.

325
00:20:41,451 --> 00:20:44,769
The trap is treating a
summary as a perfect record.

326
00:20:46,569 --> 00:20:47,428
Quick check.

327
00:20:47,778 --> 00:20:53,272
Why does compact drop the old
messages only after the summary call returns?

328
00:20:59,472 --> 00:21:03,154
Because if the call
fails, no conversation is lost.

329
00:21:04,954 --> 00:21:06,043
Let's recap.

330
00:21:06,393 --> 00:21:10,104
Summary memory turns old
turns into a running summary.

331
00:21:10,704 --> 00:21:12,915
Recent turns stay word for word.

332
00:21:13,515 --> 00:21:17,540
It costs one extra call,
only when you pass a token limit.

333
00:21:18,140 --> 00:21:22,501
Summarize first, then drop, and
tell the summarizer what to keep.

334
00:21:23,101 --> 00:21:25,323
Next lesson: long-term memory.

335
00:21:25,673 --> 00:21:31,289
Facts that survive a restart and a new
day, saved by the model with tools, and

336
00:21:31,289 --> 00:21:32,945
protected by your code.

337
00:21:34,745 --> 00:21:38,374
Windows and summaries
help within one conversation.

338
00:21:38,724 --> 00:21:42,552
But tomorrow is a new
conversation, and they start empty.

339
00:21:42,902 --> 00:21:48,068
In this lesson, you'll give the agent
long-term memory: short facts about the

340
00:21:48,068 --> 00:21:52,362
learner, saved with tools, and
brought back into every new chat.

341
00:21:54,162 --> 00:21:57,117
You'll decide what
belongs in long-term memory.

342
00:21:57,717 --> 00:21:59,753
Save, recall and forget facts.

343
00:22:00,353 --> 00:22:03,042
Let the model manage
memory, through tools.

344
00:22:03,642 --> 00:22:08,471
And put the relevant facts into the
system prompt, while refusing secrets.

345
00:22:10,271 --> 00:22:12,170
Here's the picture for this lesson.

346
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:16,773
A good tutor with many students
keeps an index card for each one.

347
00:22:17,123 --> 00:22:18,384
Name: Anuj.

348
00:22:18,734 --> 00:22:20,317
Goal: AI agents.

349
00:22:20,667 --> 00:22:22,247
Finds recursion hard.

350
00:22:22,597 --> 00:22:24,682
Not a transcript of every lesson.

351
00:22:25,032 --> 00:22:27,344
Just a few facts that will matter later.

352
00:22:27,694 --> 00:22:31,238
And before each session,
the tutor glances at the card.

353
00:22:31,838 --> 00:22:35,502
The card holds a few facts
per learner, not the whole chat.

354
00:22:36,102 --> 00:22:39,280
Writing on the card
is a tool: remember fact.

355
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:44,165
Glancing at it is putting the
relevant facts into the system prompt.

356
00:22:44,765 --> 00:22:46,407
What belongs on the card?

357
00:22:46,757 --> 00:22:52,053
Facts that stay true and will help later:
who the learner is, their goals, what they

358
00:22:52,053 --> 00:22:55,458
find hard, what they
finished, how they like to learn.

359
00:22:55,808 --> 00:22:58,097
Not small talk, and never secrets.

360
00:22:59,897 --> 00:23:02,704
Let's use the
project's memory class directly.

361
00:23:03,054 --> 00:23:05,227
Code on the left, output on the right.

362
00:23:05,827 --> 00:23:11,845
We open a throwaway database that lives
only in memory, and create the long-term

363
00:23:11,845 --> 00:23:13,801
memory for one user, Anuj.

364
00:23:14,151 --> 00:23:16,988
Lesson five covers the database itself.

365
00:23:17,588 --> 00:23:19,209
We remember three facts.

366
00:23:19,559 --> 00:23:24,988
Each one gets a number, a topic, like
profile or struggle, and the date it was

367
00:23:24,988 --> 00:23:25,406
saved.

368
00:23:26,006 --> 00:23:29,048
Recall finds facts that
share words with a question.

369
00:23:29,398 --> 00:23:32,254
Can you help with
recursion, finds fact two.

370
00:23:32,604 --> 00:23:34,038
Simple word matching.

371
00:23:34,388 --> 00:23:38,811
In the RAG module, you'll replace it
with embeddings, which match meaning.

372
00:23:39,411 --> 00:23:41,720
Now we try to remember a password.

373
00:23:42,070 --> 00:23:43,223
It's refused.

374
00:23:43,573 --> 00:23:47,758
That check is in our code, so
it holds whatever the model asks.

375
00:23:48,358 --> 00:23:50,833
Forget deletes one fact, by its number.

376
00:23:51,433 --> 00:23:56,615
And for the prompt picks what to show
the model: always the profile, then facts

377
00:23:56,615 --> 00:23:59,566
relevant to the
message, and the newest ones.

378
00:23:59,916 --> 00:24:01,887
Not every fact, every time.

379
00:24:02,237 --> 00:24:03,812
Just what fits and helps.

380
00:24:05,612 --> 00:24:07,354
Who decides what to remember?

381
00:24:07,704 --> 00:24:09,264
The model, through tools.

382
00:24:09,614 --> 00:24:11,357
Here's the remember fact tool.

383
00:24:11,707 --> 00:24:17,378
Its description says when to use it, to
save facts, not whole messages, and never to

384
00:24:17,378 --> 00:24:18,255
save secrets.

385
00:24:18,855 --> 00:24:23,155
Recall facts searches memory,
and returns facts with their numbers.

386
00:24:23,755 --> 00:24:26,337
Forget fact needs one of those numbers.

387
00:24:26,687 --> 00:24:31,436
So forgetting is a two step plan:
recall to find the number, then forget.

388
00:24:33,236 --> 00:24:37,186
The inputs are
Pydantic models, as in Module 5.

389
00:24:37,536 --> 00:24:43,367
The topic must be one of five values,
and each one is explained, so the model knows

390
00:24:43,367 --> 00:24:44,491
which to choose.

391
00:24:45,091 --> 00:24:48,405
A fact must be between
three and two hundred characters.

392
00:24:48,755 --> 00:24:50,097
Facts, not essays.

393
00:24:50,697 --> 00:24:53,021
And a fact number must be one or more.

394
00:24:53,371 --> 00:24:57,495
Anything else becomes a tool
error the model can read and fix.

395
00:24:59,295 --> 00:25:02,672
Here's where long-term
memory meets the conversation.

396
00:25:03,022 --> 00:25:07,032
For every new message, we pick
the relevant facts, one per line.

397
00:25:07,632 --> 00:25:12,631
They go into the system prompt, under
the heading: what you remember about the

398
00:25:12,631 --> 00:25:13,143
learner.

399
00:25:13,743 --> 00:25:17,875
And when there's a running summary
from lesson three, it goes there too.

400
00:25:18,225 --> 00:25:23,600
So every request carries the three
layers: facts, summary, and recent turns.

401
00:25:25,400 --> 00:25:27,103
Let's put the layers together.

402
00:25:27,453 --> 00:25:31,934
The system prompt, with the
long-term facts: small, and always there.

403
00:25:32,534 --> 00:25:35,749
The running summary:
the gist of older turns.

404
00:25:36,349 --> 00:25:38,737
And the recent turns, word for word.

405
00:25:39,087 --> 00:25:44,375
Each layer is small, so the request
stays small, however long the relationship

406
00:25:44,375 --> 00:25:44,782
lasts.

407
00:25:46,582 --> 00:25:49,944
Now some rules, because
memory is personal data.

408
00:25:50,294 --> 00:25:52,197
First: never store secrets.

409
00:25:52,547 --> 00:25:58,462
Passwords, keys and card numbers are
refused in code, not just discouraged in the

410
00:25:58,462 --> 00:25:58,973
prompt.

411
00:25:59,573 --> 00:26:00,935
Second: store less.

412
00:26:01,285 --> 00:26:05,694
Short facts the user would
expect you to keep, not whole messages.

413
00:26:06,294 --> 00:26:07,842
Third: make it visible.

414
00:26:08,192 --> 00:26:11,461
The user can list
everything you remember about them.

415
00:26:12,061 --> 00:26:13,848
And fourth: let them delete it.

416
00:26:14,198 --> 00:26:15,857
One fact, or everything.

417
00:26:16,207 --> 00:26:19,027
Lesson five adds automatic retention.

418
00:26:20,827 --> 00:26:21,983
Pause and try.

419
00:26:22,333 --> 00:26:24,101
The learner says: morning!

420
00:26:24,451 --> 00:26:25,744
Traffic was awful.

421
00:26:26,094 --> 00:26:30,345
I finished the decorators
lesson, and I'd like shorter examples.

422
00:26:30,695 --> 00:26:34,303
My bank PIN is four
three two one, by the way.

423
00:26:34,902 --> 00:26:38,475
Which remember fact calls
should happen, and with which topic?

424
00:26:45,075 --> 00:26:46,035
Two calls.

425
00:26:46,385 --> 00:26:50,565
The learner finished the
decorators lesson, with topic progress.

426
00:26:50,915 --> 00:26:54,565
And the learner prefers
shorter examples, topic preference.

427
00:26:54,915 --> 00:26:57,388
Not the traffic: it won't matter later.

428
00:26:57,738 --> 00:26:59,242
And never the PIN.

429
00:27:01,042 --> 00:27:02,183
Interview corner.

430
00:27:02,533 --> 00:27:05,799
How would you give an
agent memory across sessions?

431
00:27:08,399 --> 00:27:12,944
A good answer: store lasting
facts for each user in a database.

432
00:27:13,294 --> 00:27:17,029
Let the model save, recall
and forget them through tools.

433
00:27:17,379 --> 00:27:20,528
Validate inputs and
block secrets in your code.

434
00:27:20,878 --> 00:27:25,758
And put the relevant facts into the
system prompt of each new conversation.

435
00:27:26,108 --> 00:27:30,358
The trap: saving every message
forever, and sending it all back.

436
00:27:30,708 --> 00:27:33,482
That's costly, slow, and a privacy risk.

437
00:27:34,082 --> 00:27:35,019
Follow-up.

438
00:27:35,369 --> 00:27:39,284
Who decides what gets
remembered: the model, or your code?

439
00:27:41,884 --> 00:27:42,573
Both.

440
00:27:42,923 --> 00:27:46,593
The model judges what's
worth saving, through tool calls.

441
00:27:46,943 --> 00:27:53,019
Your code enforces the rules: the allowed
topics, the length, no secrets, one user's

442
00:27:53,019 --> 00:27:56,274
data kept apart from
another's, and deletion.

443
00:27:56,624 --> 00:28:00,934
The trap is trusting a prompt
instruction alone to keep secrets out.

444
00:28:02,734 --> 00:28:03,592
Quick check.

445
00:28:03,942 --> 00:28:08,453
Where does the companion put
long-term facts, so the model can use them?

446
00:28:14,653 --> 00:28:16,908
In the system prompt of each request.

447
00:28:17,258 --> 00:28:19,895
The model learns nothing between calls.

448
00:28:21,695 --> 00:28:22,785
Let's recap.

449
00:28:23,135 --> 00:28:26,465
Long-term memory holds
short facts that stay true.

450
00:28:27,065 --> 00:28:31,492
The model manages it with
tools: remember, recall and forget.

451
00:28:32,092 --> 00:28:34,972
The relevant facts go
into every system prompt.

452
00:28:35,572 --> 00:28:40,529
And your code refuses secrets, and
lets users see and delete what's stored.

453
00:28:41,129 --> 00:28:43,031
Next lesson: where it's stored.

454
00:28:43,381 --> 00:28:49,533
You'll learn SQLite from scratch:
tables, safe queries, transactions, keeping users

455
00:28:49,533 --> 00:28:52,721
apart, and deleting
old data on a schedule.

456
00:28:54,532 --> 00:28:58,698
Memory that disappears when
the program closes isn't long-term.

457
00:28:59,048 --> 00:29:00,685
It needs a home on disk.

458
00:29:01,035 --> 00:29:07,277
In this lesson you'll learn SQLite from
scratch: tables, safe queries, transactions,

459
00:29:07,277 --> 00:29:10,993
keeping users apart, and
deleting data on purpose.

460
00:29:11,343 --> 00:29:13,908
Then you'll see how it
compares with PostgreSQL.

461
00:29:15,708 --> 00:29:19,875
You'll create tables, and
insert and read rows, with SQLite.

462
00:29:20,475 --> 00:29:23,612
Write queries that are
safe from SQL injection.

463
00:29:24,212 --> 00:29:27,035
Use transactions,
which are all or nothing.

464
00:29:27,635 --> 00:29:31,790
And keep users apart, and
delete old or unwanted data.

465
00:29:33,590 --> 00:29:35,465
First, what is a database?

466
00:29:35,815 --> 00:29:38,573
Picture a filing
cabinet with strict drawers.

467
00:29:38,923 --> 00:29:44,271
Each drawer is a table: one for
facts, one for sessions, one for messages.

468
00:29:44,621 --> 00:29:50,518
Each card in a drawer is a row, and
every card has the same fields, called columns.

469
00:29:50,868 --> 00:29:55,942
SQL is the language you use to
ask the clerk: give me Anuj's facts.

470
00:29:56,542 --> 00:30:01,511
SQLite is a full SQL
database that lives in a single file.

471
00:30:02,111 --> 00:30:03,686
It's built into Python.

472
00:30:04,036 --> 00:30:09,095
Import sqlite three, and there's
nothing to install, and no server to run.

473
00:30:09,695 --> 00:30:15,063
It survives restarts, and it handles
crashes safely, thanks to transactions.

474
00:30:15,663 --> 00:30:18,395
Why not the JSON file from Module 5?

475
00:30:18,745 --> 00:30:25,186
A JSON file is rewritten whole on every
save, has no queries, and gets messy with

476
00:30:25,186 --> 00:30:26,060
many users.

477
00:30:26,410 --> 00:30:32,480
A database finds one user's rows
quickly, changes them safely, and grows with you.

478
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:35,881
Let's learn it by running it.

479
00:30:36,231 --> 00:30:38,403
Code on the left, output on the right.

480
00:30:39,003 --> 00:30:41,662
Connect opens a database, or creates it.

481
00:30:42,012 --> 00:30:44,377
Here it lives in memory, for the demo.

482
00:30:44,727 --> 00:30:46,929
In real code, it's a file path.

483
00:30:47,279 --> 00:30:53,269
Create table makes a table called
facts, with an ID, a user ID and the text.

484
00:30:53,869 --> 00:30:55,333
Insert adds rows.

485
00:30:55,683 --> 00:30:57,344
Notice the question marks.

486
00:30:57,694 --> 00:31:00,731
The values travel
separately from the SQL text.

487
00:31:01,081 --> 00:31:02,710
Hold on to that idea.

488
00:31:03,060 --> 00:31:06,406
And the with block makes
both inserts one transaction.

489
00:31:07,006 --> 00:31:08,546
Select reads rows.

490
00:31:08,896 --> 00:31:14,761
Where user ID equals Anuj means
Anuj sees only Anuj's row, not Riya's.

491
00:31:15,111 --> 00:31:17,634
That's user isolation, in one line.

492
00:31:18,234 --> 00:31:19,316
Now an attack.

493
00:31:19,666 --> 00:31:24,049
This value tries to rewrite
the query, so it matches every row.

494
00:31:24,399 --> 00:31:30,050
Because it's passed as a parameter, it's
treated as plain data, a very strange user

495
00:31:30,050 --> 00:31:31,888
name, and it finds nothing.

496
00:31:32,488 --> 00:31:33,956
Next, a transaction.

497
00:31:34,306 --> 00:31:38,978
We delete Anuj's rows, and then
crash halfway, before the block ends.

498
00:31:39,578 --> 00:31:40,993
And still two facts.

499
00:31:41,343 --> 00:31:43,607
The error rolled the whole block back.

500
00:31:43,957 --> 00:31:45,130
All or nothing.

501
00:31:45,730 --> 00:31:47,268
Last, the user version.

502
00:31:47,618 --> 00:31:50,811
It's a number SQLite
stores in the file for you.

503
00:31:51,161 --> 00:31:56,655
Use it as your schema version, so a
newer program can recognise an old file.

504
00:31:58,455 --> 00:32:00,671
Here's why the question marks matter.

505
00:32:01,021 --> 00:32:06,625
If you paste a value into the SQL with
an f-string, the value becomes part of the

506
00:32:06,625 --> 00:32:07,040
query.

507
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:12,666
This input turns the condition
into: user is x, or one equals one.

508
00:32:13,016 --> 00:32:17,422
That's true for every row, so
one user reads everybody's facts.

509
00:32:17,772 --> 00:32:19,495
That's SQL injection.

510
00:32:20,095 --> 00:32:25,261
With a parameter, the database
receives the SQL and the value separately.

511
00:32:25,611 --> 00:32:28,167
The value can never change the query.

512
00:32:28,517 --> 00:32:30,042
Always use parameters.

513
00:32:30,392 --> 00:32:35,951
In an agent, this matters even more,
because values may come from model output.

514
00:32:37,751 --> 00:32:39,363
Here's the project's schema.

515
00:32:39,713 --> 00:32:42,506
Facts: the long-term
memory from lesson four.

516
00:32:42,856 --> 00:32:44,910
Every fact has a user ID.

517
00:32:45,510 --> 00:32:50,927
Sessions: one row per conversation,
with its running summary from lesson three.

518
00:32:51,277 --> 00:32:54,730
The key is the user and
the session ID together.

519
00:32:55,330 --> 00:32:58,687
Messages: the
conversation, one message per row.

520
00:32:59,037 --> 00:33:04,975
The content is stored as JSON text,
because it can be a string or a list of blocks,

521
00:33:04,975 --> 00:33:05,976
like tool use.

522
00:33:06,576 --> 00:33:10,215
And this foreign key says
every message belongs to a session.

523
00:33:10,565 --> 00:33:15,769
On delete cascade means: delete a
session, and its messages are deleted

524
00:33:15,769 --> 00:33:16,796
automatically.

525
00:33:17,396 --> 00:33:22,484
Indexes make looking up one
user's rows fast, even with many users.

526
00:33:24,284 --> 00:33:28,333
Saving a session after each
turn happens in one transaction.

527
00:33:28,933 --> 00:33:34,886
First an upsert: insert the session, or
if it already exists, update its summary and

528
00:33:34,886 --> 00:33:36,728
the date it was last used.

529
00:33:37,328 --> 00:33:39,264
Then replace its messages.

530
00:33:39,614 --> 00:33:44,961
If the program crashes in the middle,
the transaction rolls back, and the old

531
00:33:44,961 --> 00:33:45,933
version stays.

532
00:33:46,283 --> 00:33:48,102
Never half a conversation.

533
00:33:49,902 --> 00:33:53,844
Memory is personal data,
so deleting it must be easy.

534
00:33:54,194 --> 00:34:00,115
Purge is a retention rule: delete this
user's sessions not used for so many days.

535
00:34:00,465 --> 00:34:03,199
Their messages go
too, through the cascade.

536
00:34:03,799 --> 00:34:10,114
And delete user removes every fact and
every session of one user, and nobody else's,

537
00:34:10,114 --> 00:34:11,543
in one transaction.

538
00:34:11,893 --> 00:34:14,552
In the project,
that's the command delete me.

539
00:34:16,352 --> 00:34:17,907
When do you need PostgreSQL?

540
00:34:18,257 --> 00:34:21,244
SQLite is a file inside your program.

541
00:34:21,594 --> 00:34:23,904
PostgreSQL is a server you connect to.

542
00:34:24,504 --> 00:34:27,598
SQLite is best for one app on one machine.

543
00:34:27,948 --> 00:34:31,919
PostgreSQL, for many app
servers sharing data with many users.

544
00:34:32,519 --> 00:34:35,280
SQLite allows one writer at a time.

545
00:34:35,630 --> 00:34:37,633
PostgreSQL handles many at once.

546
00:34:38,233 --> 00:34:41,364
In Python, SQLite uses question marks.

547
00:34:41,714 --> 00:34:46,641
With PostgreSQL, through the
psycopg library, placeholders are percent s.

548
00:34:46,991 --> 00:34:49,331
Most of the SQL stays the same.

549
00:34:51,131 --> 00:34:52,287
Pause and try.

550
00:34:52,637 --> 00:34:58,433
There's a sessions table with a user
ID, an ID, and the date last updated.

551
00:34:58,783 --> 00:35:03,438
Write the query for Riya's
sessions last used before October first.

552
00:35:04,038 --> 00:35:06,554
Write the SQL and the Python call.

553
00:35:06,904 --> 00:35:08,396
Where do the values go?

554
00:35:14,996 --> 00:35:21,549
conn dot execute, with: select id from
sessions where user ID equals question mark

555
00:35:21,549 --> 00:35:24,666
and updated is less than question mark.

556
00:35:25,016 --> 00:35:27,165
Then a tuple: riya, and the date.

557
00:35:27,515 --> 00:35:31,330
The values go in the
tuple, never in the SQL text.

558
00:35:31,680 --> 00:35:34,297
ISO dates compare correctly as text.

559
00:35:36,097 --> 00:35:37,238
Interview corner.

560
00:35:37,588 --> 00:35:40,922
What is SQL injection,
and how do you prevent it?

561
00:35:43,522 --> 00:35:49,576
A good answer: it's input that becomes
part of the SQL text and changes the query.

562
00:35:49,926 --> 00:35:55,585
You prevent it with parameterised
queries, using question marks or percent s.

563
00:35:55,935 --> 00:36:00,605
The driver sends the values
separately, so they're always treated as data.

564
00:36:00,955 --> 00:36:03,428
Never build SQL with f-strings.

565
00:36:03,778 --> 00:36:06,227
The trap is escaping quotes by hand.

566
00:36:06,577 --> 00:36:08,121
Parameters are the fix.

567
00:36:08,721 --> 00:36:09,658
Follow-up.

568
00:36:10,008 --> 00:36:13,270
How do you keep one user's
memory away from another's?

569
00:36:15,870 --> 00:36:20,433
Every table has a user ID,
and every query filters by it.

570
00:36:20,783 --> 00:36:24,922
The user ID comes from your
app's login, never from the model.

571
00:36:25,272 --> 00:36:29,966
And you test it: user B
can't read or delete user A's rows.

572
00:36:30,316 --> 00:36:35,328
The trap is letting the model
pass the user ID as a tool argument.

573
00:36:35,678 --> 00:36:38,778
Then a clever prompt
could reach someone else's data.

574
00:36:40,578 --> 00:36:41,437
Quick check.

575
00:36:41,787 --> 00:36:47,308
A transaction deletes a session's
messages, and then the program crashes before the

576
00:36:47,308 --> 00:36:48,040
block ends.

577
00:36:48,390 --> 00:36:49,872
What's in the database?

578
00:36:56,072 --> 00:36:59,002
Everything as it was
before the transaction.

579
00:36:59,352 --> 00:37:00,525
All or nothing.

580
00:37:02,325 --> 00:37:03,415
Let's recap.

581
00:37:03,765 --> 00:37:08,943
SQLite stores tables of rows in
one file, and it's built into Python.

582
00:37:09,543 --> 00:37:11,069
Always use parameters.

583
00:37:11,419 --> 00:37:13,955
Values are data, never SQL.

584
00:37:14,555 --> 00:37:16,476
Transactions are all or nothing.

585
00:37:17,076 --> 00:37:22,531
And every row has a user ID, with
purge and delete built in from day one.

586
00:37:23,131 --> 00:37:27,557
Next lesson, the project: the
Persistent AI Study Companion.

587
00:37:27,907 --> 00:37:33,762
We put windows, summaries, long-term
facts and SQLite together into one agent.

588
00:37:35,577 --> 00:37:36,980
It's project time.

589
00:37:37,330 --> 00:37:41,383
You've learned four kinds of
memory, and how to store them safely.

590
00:37:41,733 --> 00:37:43,416
Now we put them together.

591
00:37:43,766 --> 00:37:49,388
Over two lessons, you'll build a study
companion that remembers you across days,

592
00:37:49,388 --> 00:37:54,940
keeps its conversations small, and
lets you see and delete everything it knows.

593
00:37:56,740 --> 00:37:58,153
Here's what we're building.

594
00:37:58,503 --> 00:38:04,433
A study companion you talk to at the
command line: one message with ask, or a whole

595
00:38:04,433 --> 00:38:05,148
chat loop.

596
00:38:05,748 --> 00:38:11,153
It saves lasting facts with tools:
your name, your goals, what you find hard.

597
00:38:11,753 --> 00:38:17,336
Conversations are saved and resume
where you left off, and old turns fold into a

598
00:38:17,336 --> 00:38:18,452
running summary.

599
00:38:19,052 --> 00:38:23,744
Everything lives in SQLite,
with each user's data kept apart.

600
00:38:24,344 --> 00:38:29,833
You can see everything it knows, forget
a fact, purge old chats, or delete it all.

601
00:38:30,183 --> 00:38:33,525
And there are forty-one
tests, with an offline mode.

602
00:38:35,325 --> 00:38:36,455
Here's the plan.

603
00:38:36,805 --> 00:38:42,538
The command line opens the companion for
one user and one session, and passes each

604
00:38:42,538 --> 00:38:44,006
message to the agent.

605
00:38:44,606 --> 00:38:49,300
The agent keeps the conversation:
recent turns, and the running summary.

606
00:38:49,650 --> 00:38:52,797
That's short-term memory,
from lessons two and three.

607
00:38:53,397 --> 00:38:57,453
The model reaches long-term
memory only through three tools.

608
00:38:57,803 --> 00:39:04,435
Memory dot py holds the facts, and
everything is stored by db dot py, in SQLite.

609
00:39:05,035 --> 00:39:10,658
And the client talks to the real Claude
API when there's a key, or to the scripted

610
00:39:10,658 --> 00:39:11,618
fake, offline.

611
00:39:13,418 --> 00:39:17,608
Settings come from
environment variables, as in every module.

612
00:39:17,958 --> 00:39:22,899
No API key means offline mode,
and keys are never written in code.

613
00:39:23,499 --> 00:39:26,681
COMP home is the
folder for the database file.

614
00:39:27,281 --> 00:39:30,793
Keep turns: how many
recent turns stay word for word.

615
00:39:31,143 --> 00:39:35,326
And summarize at: the
estimated size that triggers a summary.

616
00:39:35,675 --> 00:39:38,153
You can tune both without touching code.

617
00:39:38,753 --> 00:39:43,289
And COMP today fixes the date,
so tests and demos are repeatable.

618
00:39:45,089 --> 00:39:48,836
The offline fake is
built to teach memory honestly.

619
00:39:49,186 --> 00:39:54,580
Like a real model, it only knows what's
in the request: the system prompt, with the

620
00:39:54,580 --> 00:39:56,920
summary and facts, and the messages.

621
00:39:57,270 --> 00:39:59,918
Drop the history, and
it can't know your name.

622
00:40:00,518 --> 00:40:02,178
It follows a fixed script.

623
00:40:02,527 --> 00:40:08,321
Phrases like my name is, or I find
something hard, become remember fact calls.

624
00:40:08,671 --> 00:40:11,041
Forget becomes recall, then forget.

625
00:40:11,641 --> 00:40:17,063
And every reply is labelled offline
demo, and every summary offline summary.

626
00:40:17,413 --> 00:40:19,983
It's never presented as model output.

627
00:40:21,783 --> 00:40:23,848
The command line opens a session.

628
00:40:24,198 --> 00:40:29,900
By default, the session is today's
date, so each day starts a fresh conversation,

629
00:40:29,900 --> 00:40:32,223
while long-term facts carry over.

630
00:40:32,823 --> 00:40:36,512
Creating the companion loads
the session from the database.

631
00:40:36,862 --> 00:40:41,213
If it has turns already, it's
resumed, and the first line says so.

632
00:40:41,813 --> 00:40:47,208
And chat is a read eval print loop:
read a line, answer, until you type bye.

633
00:40:47,558 --> 00:40:51,058
Piped input works too,
which makes demos repeatable.

634
00:40:52,858 --> 00:40:53,796
Let's run it.

635
00:40:54,146 --> 00:40:58,955
Every command is a separate
process, like closing and reopening the app.

636
00:40:59,555 --> 00:41:05,297
First, a clean start: remove the data
folder, so the database is created fresh.

637
00:41:05,897 --> 00:41:08,987
Now a first message,
in a session called mon.

638
00:41:09,337 --> 00:41:10,412
A new session.

639
00:41:10,762 --> 00:41:16,331
The model calls remember fact twice, in
one turn: a profile fact, and a struggle.

640
00:41:16,681 --> 00:41:20,888
Then a short reply, and the
footer shows the conversation size.

641
00:41:21,488 --> 00:41:23,735
A second message, in the same session.

642
00:41:24,085 --> 00:41:27,264
It's resumed with one
turn, and a goal is saved.

643
00:41:27,864 --> 00:41:33,349
The memory command shows everything
remembered about you: three facts, with topics

644
00:41:33,349 --> 00:41:34,017
and dates.

645
00:41:34,367 --> 00:41:35,932
Visible, as promised.

646
00:41:36,532 --> 00:41:39,488
And the chat loop,
continuing the same session.

647
00:41:39,838 --> 00:41:41,273
What did we talk about?

648
00:41:41,623 --> 00:41:46,938
It can see both earlier turns, because
the session was loaded from the database.

649
00:41:48,738 --> 00:41:49,895
Pause and try.

650
00:41:50,245 --> 00:41:54,408
Tomorrow, you run ask, what's
my name, without a session option.

651
00:41:55,008 --> 00:41:56,504
Which session is used?

652
00:41:56,854 --> 00:41:58,148
What's in the request?

653
00:41:58,498 --> 00:41:59,801
And can it answer?

654
00:42:06,401 --> 00:42:09,854
The session is tomorrow's
date, so it's new and empty.

655
00:42:10,204 --> 00:42:15,530
But the system prompt still carries the
long-term facts, including: the learner's

656
00:42:15,530 --> 00:42:16,384
name is Anuj.

657
00:42:16,734 --> 00:42:18,707
So yes, it answers Anuj.

658
00:42:19,057 --> 00:42:23,543
That's the difference between
conversation memory and long-term memory.

659
00:42:25,343 --> 00:42:26,484
Interview corner.

660
00:42:26,834 --> 00:42:30,474
What's the difference between
a session, and long-term memory?

661
00:42:33,074 --> 00:42:39,197
A good answer: a session is one
conversation, its turns and its summary, which you

662
00:42:39,197 --> 00:42:40,766
can resume by its ID.

663
00:42:41,116 --> 00:42:45,585
Long-term memory is facts about
the user, shared by all sessions.

664
00:42:45,935 --> 00:42:49,919
A new session starts with an
empty history, but the same facts.

665
00:42:50,269 --> 00:42:54,667
The trap is keeping everything in
one conversation that grows forever.

666
00:42:55,267 --> 00:42:56,204
Follow-up.

667
00:42:56,554 --> 00:43:01,162
How do you test an agent's
memory without paying for API calls?

668
00:43:03,762 --> 00:43:09,930
Run the real agent, the real tools and
a real database against a fake API that

669
00:43:09,930 --> 00:43:15,150
speaks the same protocol, with a
temporary database for each test.

670
00:43:15,500 --> 00:43:20,110
Then check the facts saved, the
requests sent, and the sessions reloaded.

671
00:43:20,460 --> 00:43:25,692
The trap is mocking the agent's own
methods, so the real loop is never tested.

672
00:43:27,492 --> 00:43:28,350
Quick check.

673
00:43:28,700 --> 00:43:33,888
In the companion, which part decides
that, I find recursion hard, is worth

674
00:43:33,888 --> 00:43:34,729
remembering?

675
00:43:40,929 --> 00:43:43,282
The model, by calling remember fact.

676
00:43:43,632 --> 00:43:45,775
Your code then checks it, and stores it.

677
00:43:47,575 --> 00:43:48,664
Let's recap.

678
00:43:49,014 --> 00:43:54,871
The layers: the command line, the
agent, the conversation, and tools, memory and

679
00:43:54,871 --> 00:43:55,384
SQLite.

680
00:43:55,984 --> 00:44:00,354
Settings come from the
environment, and it runs offline without a key.

681
00:44:00,954 --> 00:44:04,665
The fake is honest: it only
knows what's in the request.

682
00:44:05,265 --> 00:44:10,278
Sessions resume from the database,
and facts are shared by all sessions.

683
00:44:10,878 --> 00:44:14,149
Next lesson: the
agent's turn, line by line.

684
00:44:14,499 --> 00:44:20,479
Then a two-day demo, privacy features on
purpose, the tests, and the module wrap-up.

685
00:44:22,297 --> 00:44:23,357
Welcome back.

686
00:44:23,707 --> 00:44:27,118
The storage, the memory
and the command line are ready.

687
00:44:27,468 --> 00:44:33,379
Now we read the agent's turn line by
line, run a demo across two days, use every

688
00:44:33,379 --> 00:44:37,074
privacy feature on
purpose, and look at the tests.

689
00:44:37,424 --> 00:44:42,851
Then we wrap up the module with
interview questions, a quiz, and your extension

690
00:44:42,851 --> 00:44:43,538
challenge.

691
00:44:45,338 --> 00:44:46,966
First, the system prompt.

692
00:44:47,316 --> 00:44:53,606
As in Module 3, it explains why: the
companion remembers through long-term memory,

693
00:44:53,606 --> 00:44:56,827
because a conversation alone is forgotten.

694
00:44:57,427 --> 00:45:02,917
It says what's worth saving, one fact
per call, and to use what it remembers to

695
00:45:02,917 --> 00:45:05,488
personalise explanations and quizzes.

696
00:45:06,088 --> 00:45:07,759
And never to save secrets.

697
00:45:08,109 --> 00:45:11,243
Remember, the prompt
asks, but the code enforces.

698
00:45:13,043 --> 00:45:14,234
Here's one turn.

699
00:45:14,584 --> 00:45:18,373
We remember how many messages
there were, then run the turn.

700
00:45:18,973 --> 00:45:25,223
If the turn didn't end with an answer, a
refusal, a cut off reply, too many steps,

701
00:45:25,223 --> 00:45:26,976
we remove its messages.

702
00:45:27,326 --> 00:45:28,968
Never save half a turn.

703
00:45:29,568 --> 00:45:35,128
After a good answer, compact folds old
turns into the summary, if we're over the

704
00:45:35,128 --> 00:45:35,545
limit.

705
00:45:35,895 --> 00:45:37,180
That's lesson three.

706
00:45:37,780 --> 00:45:41,615
An API error undoes the
turn too, and is reported.

707
00:45:42,215 --> 00:45:47,484
And finally, the whole session is
saved in one transaction, as in lesson five.

708
00:45:49,284 --> 00:45:54,595
Inside the turn: add the user's
message, and build the system prompt for this

709
00:45:54,595 --> 00:45:57,975
message, with the
relevant facts and the summary.

710
00:45:58,575 --> 00:46:00,472
Then the loop from Module 4.

711
00:46:00,822 --> 00:46:06,815
Each reply is stored as plain JSON
blocks, so it can be saved to the database, and

712
00:46:06,815 --> 00:46:08,276
sent again tomorrow.

713
00:46:08,876 --> 00:46:11,950
No tool calls means
the model has answered.

714
00:46:12,550 --> 00:46:19,116
Otherwise, every memory tool call runs,
a refused secret becomes an error result,

715
00:46:19,116 --> 00:46:23,899
and all the results go back
in one message, as in Module 5.

716
00:46:25,699 --> 00:46:26,760
Now the demo.

717
00:46:27,110 --> 00:46:29,943
It's the next day: a
new session called tue.

718
00:46:30,293 --> 00:46:33,369
The conversation from
mon isn't sent at all.

719
00:46:33,969 --> 00:46:35,051
What's my name?

720
00:46:35,401 --> 00:46:36,921
A new, empty session.

721
00:46:37,271 --> 00:46:41,759
But the answer is Anuj, from
long-term memory in the system prompt.

722
00:46:42,359 --> 00:46:43,152
Quiz me.

723
00:46:43,502 --> 00:46:48,607
It remembered that I find recursion
hard, so the question is about recursion.

724
00:46:48,957 --> 00:46:51,640
That's what memory is for: personal help.

725
00:46:52,240 --> 00:46:54,888
Now, forget that I find recursion hard.

726
00:46:55,238 --> 00:47:00,330
Two steps: recall finds the fact and
its number, then forget fact deletes it.

727
00:47:00,930 --> 00:47:02,073
And a password.

728
00:47:02,423 --> 00:47:05,700
The model tried to save
it, and our code refused.

729
00:47:06,050 --> 00:47:09,165
The error went back to
the model as an observation.

730
00:47:10,965 --> 00:47:13,929
Another user, Riya,
asks the same question.

731
00:47:14,279 --> 00:47:15,772
She doesn't get my name.

732
00:47:16,122 --> 00:47:18,234
Every query filters by user.

733
00:47:18,834 --> 00:47:22,008
Now we lower the summary
limit, to see compaction.

734
00:47:22,358 --> 00:47:25,634
The footer says: two
old messages summarized.

735
00:47:26,234 --> 00:47:28,384
History shows the session as stored.

736
00:47:28,734 --> 00:47:30,419
On top, the running summary.

737
00:47:30,769 --> 00:47:33,282
Then the recent turns, word for word.

738
00:47:33,632 --> 00:47:35,890
We show the first and last lines here.

739
00:47:36,490 --> 00:47:42,509
Sessions lists my conversations: mon and
tue, with message counts, and tue now has a

740
00:47:42,509 --> 00:47:43,082
summary.

741
00:47:43,682 --> 00:47:44,599
Retention.

742
00:47:44,949 --> 00:47:50,125
Pretend it's January: purge
deletes sessions not used for thirty days.

743
00:47:50,475 --> 00:47:52,809
Both are gone, with their messages.

744
00:47:53,409 --> 00:47:59,092
And delete me asks first, then deletes
every fact and session of mine, and nobody

745
00:47:59,092 --> 00:47:59,583
else's.

746
00:48:01,383 --> 00:48:07,562
The tests run the real agent, tools and
SQLite against the fake model, with a new

747
00:48:07,562 --> 00:48:10,156
temporary database for every test.

748
00:48:10,506 --> 00:48:16,194
This one proves the point of lesson one:
in another session, without the history or

749
00:48:16,194 --> 00:48:18,798
a saved fact, the name can't be known.

750
00:48:19,398 --> 00:48:25,317
And this one proves long-term memory: a
new session starts with no messages, still

751
00:48:25,317 --> 00:48:30,730
answers Anuj, and the request really
carried the fact in its system prompt.

752
00:48:32,530 --> 00:48:38,206
Forty-one tests: memory, the database,
the conversation, the tools, the agent and

753
00:48:38,206 --> 00:48:39,398
the command line.

754
00:48:39,748 --> 00:48:42,443
All passing, with no key and no network.

755
00:48:43,043 --> 00:48:46,727
And mypy, in strict
mode, is happy with every type.

756
00:48:48,527 --> 00:48:49,755
Common problems.

757
00:48:50,105 --> 00:48:55,656
It forgets in the middle of a chat: a
message or reply isn't appended to the history

758
00:48:55,656 --> 00:48:56,251
you send.

759
00:48:56,851 --> 00:49:02,373
A four hundred error about a tool result
without a tool use: you trimmed between a

760
00:49:02,373 --> 00:49:02,710
pair.

761
00:49:03,060 --> 00:49:04,856
Cut only where a turn starts.

762
00:49:05,456 --> 00:49:09,912
Another user's facts appear: a
query is missing the user filter.

763
00:49:10,262 --> 00:49:12,033
Write a test for isolation.

764
00:49:12,633 --> 00:49:16,283
And database is locked:
two programs writing at once.

765
00:49:16,633 --> 00:49:20,852
Keep transactions short, or
move to PostgreSQL for many writers.

766
00:49:22,652 --> 00:49:23,793
Interview corner.

767
00:49:24,143 --> 00:49:28,837
Design memory for a tutoring
agent used by thousands of students.

768
00:49:31,437 --> 00:49:35,281
A good answer: one set of
rows per student, in PostgreSQL.

769
00:49:35,631 --> 00:49:39,895
For each session, a window of
recent turns and a running summary.

770
00:49:40,245 --> 00:49:45,648
Long-term facts saved with tools, and
the relevant ones put in the system prompt.

771
00:49:45,998 --> 00:49:52,275
Secrets refused in code, a retention
rule, a delete my data command, and tests for

772
00:49:52,275 --> 00:49:53,040
isolation.

773
00:49:53,390 --> 00:49:56,701
And at scale,
embeddings to find relevant facts.

774
00:49:57,051 --> 00:50:02,726
The trap: one giant shared history,
or letting the model choose the user ID.

775
00:50:03,326 --> 00:50:04,262
Follow-up.

776
00:50:04,612 --> 00:50:07,157
A user asks you to delete their data.

777
00:50:07,507 --> 00:50:08,650
What must happen?

778
00:50:11,250 --> 00:50:16,568
Delete their facts, their sessions and
their messages, and backups and logs too,

779
00:50:16,568 --> 00:50:18,229
according to your policy.

780
00:50:18,579 --> 00:50:21,318
Confirm it, and make
sure nothing loads it again.

781
00:50:21,668 --> 00:50:26,921
In code, that's one transaction
filtered by the user, with cascades for the

782
00:50:26,921 --> 00:50:28,392
messages, and a test.

783
00:50:28,742 --> 00:50:33,330
The trap is deleting the facts, but
forgetting the stored conversations.

784
00:50:35,130 --> 00:50:37,013
Let's recap the whole module.

785
00:50:37,363 --> 00:50:39,014
The API is stateless.

786
00:50:39,364 --> 00:50:41,853
All memory is data your code sends.

787
00:50:42,453 --> 00:50:47,562
Short-term memory is a window of
recent turns, cut where a turn starts.

788
00:50:48,162 --> 00:50:52,135
Summary memory folds old
turns into a running summary.

789
00:50:52,735 --> 00:50:58,381
Long-term memory is facts saved with
tools, with the relevant ones in the system

790
00:50:58,381 --> 00:50:58,875
prompt.

791
00:50:59,475 --> 00:51:05,675
And SQLite stores it all, with
parameters, transactions, a user ID on every row,

792
00:51:05,675 --> 00:51:07,535
retention, and deletion.

793
00:51:09,335 --> 00:51:11,231
Module quiz, question one.

794
00:51:11,581 --> 00:51:15,869
Why does a chatbot remember
earlier messages in a conversation?

795
00:51:22,069 --> 00:51:25,650
Because your code resends
the history with each request.

796
00:51:26,000 --> 00:51:28,032
The API itself is stateless.

797
00:51:29,832 --> 00:51:30,922
Question two.

798
00:51:31,272 --> 00:51:34,152
Where must a sliding
window cut the conversation?

799
00:51:40,352 --> 00:51:43,937
Where a turn starts: a
user message with plain text.

800
00:51:44,287 --> 00:51:46,032
That keeps tool pairs together.

801
00:51:47,832 --> 00:51:48,963
Question three.

802
00:51:49,313 --> 00:51:51,830
What's the main
trade-off of summary memory?

803
00:51:58,030 --> 00:52:01,586
Details can be lost,
and it costs an extra call.

804
00:52:03,386 --> 00:52:04,500
Question four.

805
00:52:04,850 --> 00:52:08,398
The model calls remember
fact with a user's password.

806
00:52:08,748 --> 00:52:09,813
What should happen?

807
00:52:16,013 --> 00:52:19,312
Your code refuses it,
and returns a tool error.

808
00:52:19,662 --> 00:52:21,846
Prompts guide, code enforces.

809
00:52:23,646 --> 00:52:25,010
And question five.

810
00:52:25,360 --> 00:52:28,607
Which of these queries
is safe from SQL injection?

811
00:52:34,807 --> 00:52:35,415
B.

812
00:52:35,765 --> 00:52:39,206
It's the only one that
passes the value as a parameter.

813
00:52:39,556 --> 00:52:42,125
The others paste it into the SQL text.

814
00:52:43,925 --> 00:52:46,332
Now some practice, from easy to hard.

815
00:52:46,682 --> 00:52:48,028
Pause and try them.

816
00:52:48,378 --> 00:52:51,941
The answers are in the
module's quiz dot md file.

817
00:52:52,541 --> 00:52:53,179
One.

818
00:52:53,529 --> 00:52:58,990
Write a window by tokens function,
which keeps recent turns up to a token budget.

819
00:52:59,590 --> 00:53:00,212
Two.

820
00:53:00,562 --> 00:53:04,651
From: I'm Sam, I'm tired
today, I passed my Python exam.

821
00:53:05,001 --> 00:53:06,661
Which facts should be saved?

822
00:53:07,261 --> 00:53:07,958
Three.

823
00:53:08,308 --> 00:53:11,388
Write the SQL to
count each user's messages.

824
00:53:11,988 --> 00:53:13,641
And four, a scenario.

825
00:53:13,991 --> 00:53:16,856
A customer support
bot remembers customers.

826
00:53:17,206 --> 00:53:20,261
Design its memory, and its privacy rules.

827
00:53:28,061 --> 00:53:29,668
Your extension challenge.

828
00:53:30,018 --> 00:53:34,699
Pick one or more, and write a
test for each, using the fake API.

829
00:53:35,299 --> 00:53:40,922
Add export and import commands, that
save and load your data as a JSON file.

830
00:53:41,522 --> 00:53:46,710
Make some facts expire, for example
struggle facts older than sixty days.

831
00:53:47,310 --> 00:53:50,611
Size the window by
tokens, instead of turns.

832
00:53:51,211 --> 00:53:56,957
Or swap SQLite for PostgreSQL, with
psycopg, and keep the same tests passing.

833
00:53:58,757 --> 00:54:03,959
These are the official documentation
pages for this module, including Claude's

834
00:54:03,959 --> 00:54:08,360
ready-made memory tool, which
you can compare with what you built.

835
00:54:08,710 --> 00:54:13,317
Links are in the description, with the
full source code and the question bank.

836
00:54:19,917 --> 00:54:21,748
That's Module 6 complete.

837
00:54:22,098 --> 00:54:27,934
Your agent now remembers: within a chat,
across days, and only what it should, with

838
00:54:27,934 --> 00:54:29,974
every user's data kept apart.

839
00:54:30,324 --> 00:54:36,310
In Module 7, we make agents reliable and
efficient: retries, caching, budgets, and

840
00:54:36,310 --> 00:54:37,844
tracing what they do.

841
00:54:38,193 --> 00:54:39,136
See you there.
